{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>Lest they break through, etc.</b> The meaning is: lest they think in their minds that they will behold the Blessed One even if they die, in the manner of what is said (Psalms 63:4): ״טוֹב חַסְדְּךָ מֵחַיִּים״ — and their death itself is their life. This is what is said <b>lest they break through</b> — meaning, they will consent to their own destruction — <b>to YHWH to see</b>, meaning, as though it said ״to see toward YHWH.״ And His saying <b>and many shall fall from it</b> means their fall will be a great fall, beyond that their death itself will be for them fall upon fall. Or it may intend: since His light, may He be blessed, fills all space, when they come to see, HaShem will need to withdraw that light so that they not gaze upon it, and this is what is said <b>lest they break through to YHWH</b> — meaning, toward His light, because of their seeing. And this declares that they would not attain to see even if they came to see, and therefore Scripture did not say ״lest they see,״ for they would not attain it as mentioned, but rather they would think in their minds to see. Now perhaps you will say there is here neither the prospect of benefit nor of harm — therefore He said <b>and many shall fall, etc.</b>, meaning, beyond the fact that they will not see, there is further the prospect of harm. And He was precise to say <b>many</b>, meaning: even if it be the greatest among them, one should not say that I am concerned only about the multitude of the people, but the great ones of Israel who are worthy of the indwelling of the Shekhinah — I am not concerned — therefore Scripture says: ״וְנָפַל מִמֶּנּוּ רָב.״"], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:30:20.574673020Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}